{
  "dom_id": "step-q19294",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14236308/assets/8d531960f0aa1444b614279f1ab82d8cc01904580e44be331f42d3d28266e1a9.png)\n\nWhat is the definition of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?",
  "worked_solution": "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,4],$ we have a line with a slope of $1$ and $y$-intercept $0.$ So the graph is the line $y=x$ that ends at $(4,4),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(4,\\infty ),$ we have a horizontal line that passes through the point $(4,2).$ So the graph is the line $y=2$ that starts at $(4,2),$ excluding this point.\n\nTherefore, the function is defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x,\\, & x\\le 4 \\\\ 2,\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
